I recently completed New Jersey's Motorcycle Rider Training (MRT) course — the state-approved way to get your motorcycle endorsement without having to take the DMV road test separately. Passing the course waives it, which is the main reason most people take this route instead of going straight to the MVC.

A quick rundown for anyone considering it: the course runs over a weekend, mixes classroom time with hands-on riding on a closed course, and is built for people who are genuinely new to riding — no prior experience assumed. It covers the fundamentals: clutch control, braking, low-speed maneuvering, cornering, and enough of the "why" behind each skill that it actually sticks. By the end, you're tested on both the material and the riding itself.

Next step for me is straightforward: add the endorsement to my license. No road test required — the course certificate takes care of that.

**On gear.** Since finishing the course, I've been slowly building out a proper kit, and my filter has been simple: prioritize safety certification over brand name. Concretely, that means looking for:

- **Helmets** rated to SNELL M2025 or ECE 22.06
- **Jackets and gloves** rated CE Level 2, which is the higher of the two protection tiers for abrasion and impact

A few pieces I've landed on so far — an Oxford Nexus 1.0 glove (CE Level 2) as a solid safety pick, and a WICKED STOCK Enduro mesh jacket for warm-weather riding with good visibility. Helmet sizing turned out to matter more than I expected — I'd initially guessed my size wrong and had to size down to a Medium once I actually measured my head circumference properly.

More to come here as I put actual miles in. If I start recording rides, I'll post those too.
